  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "JavaScript Arithmetic Operators"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Arithmetic operators perform arithmetic on numbers (literals or variables)."
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "headers": [
    "Operator",
    "Description"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "+",
     "Addition"
    ],
    [
     "-",
     "Subtraction"
    ],
    [
     "*",
     "Multiplication"
    ],
    [
     "**",
     "Exponentiation (ES2016)"
    ],
    [
     "/",
     "Division"
    ],
    [
     "%",
     "Modulus (Remainder)"
    ],
    [
     "++",
     "Increment"
    ],
    [
     "--",
     "Decrement"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Arithmetic Operations"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "A typical arithmetic operation operates on two numbers."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The two numbers can be literals:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 100 + 50;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_arithmetic_operation"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "or variables:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = a + b;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_arithmetic_variables"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "or expressions:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = (100 + 50) * a;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_arithmetic_expressions"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Operators and Operands"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The numbers (in an arithmetic operation) are called operands."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The operation (to be performed between the two operands) is defined by an operator."
  },
  {
   "type": "table",
   "headers": [
    "Operand",
    "Operator",
    "Operand"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "100",
     "+",
     "50"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Adding"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The addition operator (+) adds numbers:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\nlet y = 2;\n\nlet z = x + y;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_oper_add"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Subtracting"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The subtraction operator (-) subtracts numbers."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\nlet y = 2;\n\nlet z = x - y;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_oper_sub"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Multiplying"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The multiplication operator (*) multiplies numbers."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\nlet y = 2;\n\nlet z = x * y;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_oper_mult"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Dividing"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The division operator (/) divides numbers."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\nlet y = 2;\n\nlet z = x / y;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_oper_div"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Remainder"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The modulus operator (%) returns the division remainder."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\nlet y = 2;\n\nlet z = x % y;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_oper_mod"
  },
  {
   "type": "note",
   "variant": "note",
   "text": "In arithmetic, the division of two integers produces a quotient\nand a remainder.\nIn mathematics, the result of a modulo operation is the remainder of an arithmetic division."
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Incrementing"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The increment operator (++) increments numbers."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\nx++;\n\nlet z = x;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_oper_increment"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Decrementing"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The decrement operator (--) decrements numbers."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\n x--;\n\nlet z = x;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_oper_decrement"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Exponentiation"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "The exponentiation operator (**) raises the first operand to the power of the second operand."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\nlet z =\n x ** 2;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_arithmetric_exponent1"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "x ** y produces the same result as Math.pow(x,y):"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 5;\n\nlet z =\n  Math.pow(x,2);",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_arithmetric_exponent2"
  },
  {
   "type": "h2",
   "text": "Operator Precedence"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Operator precedence describes the order in which operations are performed in\nan arithmetic expression."
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 100 + 50 * 3;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_arithmetic_precedence1"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Is the result of example above the same as 150 * 3, or is it the same as 100\n+ 150?"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Is the addition or the multiplication done first?"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "As in traditional school mathematics, the multiplication is done first."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "Multiplication (*) and division (/) have higher precedence than\naddition (+) and subtraction (-)."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "And (as in school mathematics) the precedence can be changed by using\nparentheses."
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "When using parentheses, the operations inside the parentheses are computed\nfirst:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = (100 + 50) * 3;",
   "caption": "Example"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_arithmetic_precedence2"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "When many operations have the same precedence (like addition and\nsubtraction or multiplication and division), they are computed from left to\nright:"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 100 + 50 - 3;",
   "caption": "Examples"
  },
  {
   "type": "code",
   "lang": "js",
   "text": "let x = 100 / 50 * 3;",
   "caption": "Examples"
  },
  {
   "type": "tryit",
   "url": "https://www.w3schools.com/tryit.asp?filename=tryjs_arithmetic_precedence4"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "For a full list of operator precedence values go to:"
  },
  {
   "type": "p",
   "text": "JavaScript Operator Precedence Values."
  }
 ]
}